TCC Standard Dossier: Non-Visual Media Exclusion & Analytical Precision
1. The Interpretive Fallacy of Non-Visual Media
Within the TCC (The Circular Canon) framework, purely textual and audio-only formats (novels, light novels, unillustrated lore entries, radio dramas, and audiobooks) are strictly excluded from structural evaluation and tier assignment. This restriction is enforced to eliminate analytical distortion caused by subjective interpretation. • The Subjectivity Gap: Written prose is inherently superficial and abstract. A single sentence describing a "world-shattering strike" or "blinding speed" generates wildly different mental images across different readers, making objective calibration impossible. • Absence of Spatial Anchors: Without a visual frame, analysts cannot verify obstacle density, exact distances, environmental scale, or anatomical recoil. Evaluating text forces reliance on guesswork rather than empirical observation. • Minimizing Analytical Error: Visual media already requires careful contextual scrutiny to avoid misinterpretation. Eliminating non-visual sources removes the primary vector of unresolvable translation disputes, poetic ambiguity, and reader projection.
2.	Classification of Accompanying Text & Verified Potential
Written dialogue, captions, and narration appearing inside visual media (manga panels, comic boxes, or animated subtitles) are strictly subordinate to the visual frame and categorized into three distinct analytical tiers: • Confirmed Feat (Direct Execution): An action fully completed and visually depicted on screen. This is the sole basis for assigning a standard baseline tier. • Verified Potential (Corroborated Escalation): A stated ceiling of destruction (e.g., an observer stating that ongoing shockwaves will eventually destroy the universe) that is actively supported by visible, ongoing physical consequences in the frame (such as distant celestial bodies crumbling or spatial fabric destabilizing). Verified Potential is recorded as a separate conditional tag—never merged into confirmed baseline durability or single-strike output—and depends on situational escalation. • Unbacked Statement (Narrative Dialogue): Claims, boasts, or character assumptions lacking direct visual corroboration. If words do not match the visual reality on screen, they are classified strictly as dramatic dialogue, subjective guesswork, or hyperbole and carry zero analytical weight. • Contextual Verification Mandate: Analysts must cross-examine every explanatory caption against the visual action. Whether a statement constitutes valid mechanical clarification or mere character speculation is determined case-by-case based on visual evidence and situational context.
3.	Scope of Valid Media Formats
To qualify for TCC tiering and combat evaluation, a work must provide direct, continuous visual data of its entities and environments: • Permitted Formats: Live-action film and television, 2D/3D animation, anime, graphic novels, comics, manga, and video games (evaluated strictly through scripted cutscenes and canonical visual events). • Excluded Formats: Pure literature, web novels, unillustrated creepypastas, tabletop rulebooks without visual depiction, and audio-only narratives.
TCC Standard Protocol: Unconfirmed Entities & Evidentiary Limits
• Visual Frame Constraint: Background or enigmatic figures are bounded strictly by verified on-screen displays. Narrative mystery is treated as atmospheric storytelling, not combat capability. Lack of on-screen defeat does not equal immunity. • Zero Speculative Inflation: Profiles rely exclusively on concrete, completed actions. Unverified claims of "hidden cosmic supremacy" or extrapolated authority are analytically void. • Theory & Hypothesis Segregation: Unshown potential must be strictly isolated and preceded by the disclaimer: "Subjective Hypothesis: This is an unverified theory and does NOT constitute established canonical fact." TCC Directive: Restrict profiles of unconfirmed entities strictly to explicit on-screen visual feats. Pure logic rules here.
