TCC Standard Protocol: Material Science & Structural Density
1.	Abolishing Abstract Obstacle Labels
Within the TCC (The Circular Canon) framework, vague environmental classifications such as generic "wall," "room," or "structure" labels are strictly prohibited. Every breached obstacle must be evaluated through literal material composition, structural integrity, and physical thickness as visually depicted on screen. • Material Specificity: A barrier is defined by what it is physically made of (e.g., drywall, weathered timber, masonry brick, structural steel, or reinforced bunker concrete), never by its architectural room label. • Thickness & Cross-Section: Punching through a thin sheet-metal partition does not equate to penetrating a meter-thick armored bulkhead. Analysts must verify the literal depth and mass displaced in the frame.
2.	Structural Density & Resistance Calibration
Destructive output across Tiers 3–6 (Peak Human & Tactical, Building, District & Sector, and City & Metropolis) is calibrated strictly against the literal physical resistance overcome during direct execution. • Low-Density vs. High-Density Barriers: Shattering hollow plasterboard, decaying wood, or brittle stone falls within baseline tactical thresholds (Tier 3) and holds zero equivalence to fracturing high-tensile alloys or military-grade reinforced concrete (Tier 4). • Condition of Infrastructure: Pre-damaged, corroded, or structurally compromised buildings require significantly less kinetic force to collapse than intact, engineered fortifications. Environmental decay must be factored into the evaluation.
3.	Penetration Mechanics: Pulverization vs. Structural Collapse
Before assigning a destructive tier, analysts must distinguish between complete material annihilation and localized structural failure. • Load-Bearing Collapse: Toppling an entire multi-story building (Tier 4) by severing a few weak support pillars demonstrates tactical precision and localized cutting or impact force, not the raw energy output required to pulverize the entire structure simultaneously. • Non-Kinetic Bypass Exclusion: Melting a steel door with acid, phasing through walls, or teleporting matter away bypasses material density rather than overcoming it through raw Striking Force. Such feats are logged strictly as specialized utility or elemental mechanics, never as baseline physical tier multipliers. TCC Directive: Never assign a structural tier based on abstract room or building labels. Calibrate destructive output strictly by the verified material density, thickness, and failure mechanics visually demonstrated in the frame. Pure logic rules here.
TCC Standard Dossier: Kinetic Vectors & Combat Anticipation
