TCC Standard Dossier: Supremacy of Battle Intelligence (BIQ)
1.	Tactical Direction Over Raw Output
Within the TCC (The Circular Canon) framework, raw destructive capacity holds limited tactical value without the cognitive discipline required to apply it effectively. Battle Intelligence (BIQ)—encompassing combat experience, spatial awareness, pattern recognition, and real-time adaptability—functions as a primary equalizer across scale tiers. • Directed Application: A physically superior combatant lacking tactical discipline telegraphs attacks, wastes stamina on wide misses, and leaves openings after heavy strikes. • Practical Boundaries of BIQ: Tactical intelligence maximizes available tools and exploits vulnerabilities, but it does not fabricate non-existent offensive power. A tactician still requires a viable anatomical, environmental, or mechanical vector to inflict damage or achieve containment.
2.	Dismantling Physically Superior Opponents
An analytical fighter avoids direct contests of raw strength against superior scale tiers, shifting the engagement toward asymmetric mechanics: • Momentum & Structural Exploitation: Using footwork, redirection, and environmental hazards to turn a heavy attacker's own kinetic momentum and size against them. • Targeting Vector Asymmetries: Identifying and striking unarmored weak points, sensory organs, activation delays, or internal structural vulnerabilities established under the Anatomical Dissection protocol. • Psychological & Stamina Manipulation: Forcing high-output opponents to burn energy reserves on empty swings or goading arrogant fighters into predictable overextensions.
