Within the TCC (The Circular Canon) framework, linear algebraic scaling chains—such as assuming that because Combatant A defeated B, and B defeated C, Combatant A automatically surpasses C—are strictly prohibited. Fictional combat operates as a non-linear system governed by specific mechanical interactions, vector asymmetry, and tactical context rather than a static numerical ladder. • Cause-of-Victory Verification: Analysts must examine how a victory was achieved before drawing comparative conclusions. If Combatant A defeated B by exploiting a specific elemental weakness, stamina exhaustion, or environmental hazard that Combatant C does not share, the victory provides zero proof of superiority over C. • Vector Separation Over Cross-Scaling: A defeat does not erase an entity's confirmed visual feats, nor does winning a specialized matchup grant the victor the defeated opponent's unrelated physical stats.
